hls: a ranged request is counted from where it begins

A segment that is a range of bytes in a bigger file is asked for with a
request restricted to that range, and since FFmpeg 9 the segment after
it, when it follows on in the same file, is read over the same
connection: the reader seeks to where it begins, which for bytes that
follow on is no request at all.

That seek is worked out from the count the context keeps of where it
stands, and the context that is opened for a restricted request counts
from nought, though its first byte is url_offset bytes into the file. At
the end of the first segment it believed itself url_offset bytes short
of the second, and read on past that many bytes of it. With the first
segment of a fragmented MP4 file, which begins after the bytes that
describe the streams, the whole second segment was lost: a film played
its first two seconds and then its fifth.

The count is put where the request began.

--- a/libavformat/hls.c
+++ b/libavformat/hls.c
@@ -1547,6 +1547,17 @@
         ret = open_url(pls->parent, in, seg->url, &c->avio_opts, opts, &is_http);
     }
 
+    /* A HTTP request that was restricted to a part of the resource delivers
+     * its first byte from seg->url_offset, while the context opened for it
+     * counts from nought. What is read is right all the same. What went
+     * wrong is going on to the segment after it over the same connection:
+     * the seek there is worked out from the context's count, found
+     * url_offset bytes still to go, and read past that many bytes of the
+     * next segment. The count is put where the request began. */
+    if (ret == 0 && is_http && seg->size >= 0 && seg->key_type == KEY_NONE &&
+        (*in)->buf_ptr == (*in)->buf_end)
+        (*in)->pos = seg->url_offset;
+
     /* Seek to the requested position. If this was a HTTP request, the offset
      * should already be where want it to, but this allows e.g. local testing
      * without a HTTP server.
